            <ArticleTitle>Deterioration of long-term clinical results after the Elmslie-Trillat procedure for dislocation of the patella.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>861-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We examined 39 patients (45 knees) who had undergone an Elmslie-Trillat procedure for recurrent or habitual dislocation of the patella with a follow-up of more than ten years. The mean age at the time of surgery was 18.4 years; the mean follow-up was 161 months (120 to 238). Using Fulkerson's functional knee score, 41 knees (91%) had an excellent or good result at a mean follow-up of 45 months, and this was maintained in 29 (64%) at the final review. The main cause of deterioration in the clinical results was the onset or worsening of patellofemoral joint pain, not patellar instability.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
